  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nSanta Ana winds are finally diminishing this evening as easterly \nflow aloft and the offshore surface pressure gradient both continue \nto weaken. Easterly gusts 20-35 mph will continue along coastal \nslopes and through east-to-west passes through this morning. Return \nof weak onshore flow will allow for some cooling today, especially \nfor coastal areas, though highs will remain 4 to 10 degrees above \nnormal through the coastal basin.\n\nThe return of onshore flow will also herald the return of the marine \nlayer. HREF guidance introduces patchy low clouds and FG over \ncoastal waters starting early Monday morning. Onshore flow increases \nby Monday night as a closed upper low from the northwest begins to \ninfluence the region. This will allow marine layer clouds and FG to \npush into coastal areas and portions of the valleys. \n\nAs the upper low continues to slide southward, cooling will spread \nacross the region with below normal highs expected from Tuesday \nonward. Rain chances increase from west to east on Wednesday, though \nthe majority of guidance has trended a little slower with the onset \nof precipitation. Regardless, widespread light to moderate rain is \nexpected with the most likely timeframe for precip being Wednesday \nnight into Thursday. As the event draws closer, some details are \nbeginning to emerge. NBM probabilities of storm total rainfall \nexceeding 0.5\" and 1\" from the coast to the mountains are about 60-\n90% and 25-55% respectively. The higher probabilities are mainly \nfocused in the mountains and portions of the coastal basin north of \nSan Diego County. Lower rainfall totals are expected in the deserts \nwith only 30-50% and 5-25% chances of 0.5\" of rainfall in the High \nDesert and low deserts, respectively.\n\nAs the core of the low moves overhead on Thursday, temperatures will \nfall well below normal across the region. Additionally, increased \nmid-level lapse rates and vorticity will promote development of \nconvective showers. There is about a 10-15% chance for thunderstorms \nfrom the coast to the mountains at this time. If thunderstorms \ndevelop, locally heavy rainfall rates will be possible.\n\nModel solutions diverge by next weekend, but there is not much \nindication of any significant weather. The mean of the guidance \nindicates mainly zonal flow with temperatures warming back to \nslightly below normal through the weekend. In the long term, there \nis some indication that the troughing pattern will continue.",
